OUT|h6|
OUT|h6|
OUT|h6|compile [flags] <module>
OUT|h6|    ? Description: This command is an alias for `module-action module-name compile`
OUT|h6|
OUT|h6|    ? Arguments
OUT|h6|        ? module: Name of module on which to perform compile action
OUT|h6|
OUT|h6|    ? Flags:
OUT|h6|        ? -env, -e <value> : Semicolon separated paths to the environment files in json format. See wiki for details.
OUT|h6|        ? -only, -o : Only runs the specified phase(s), rather than also running predecessors.
OUT|h6|        ? -dev : Sets the DeveloperMode flag for the module's lifecycle. Key consequences of this are that ^Sources will be configured for resources in the module, and installer methods will be called with the dev mode flag set.
OUT|h6|        ? -quiet, -q : Produces minimal output. For test and verify: suppresses build/install noise while still showing the result summary and any failures.
OUT|h6|        ? -verbose, -v : Produces verbose output. For test and verify: shows per-method results in the result summary when combined with -f; without -f only the summary is shown.
OUT|h6|        ? -format, -f <json/yaml/toon> : (test and verify only) One-shot output format override for the test result summary. Overrides the persistent default set via 'config set TestReportFormat <format>'. When neither -f nor config is set, the default summary and legacy failure lines are shown. Use 'config delete TestReportFormat' to revert to default.
OUT|h6|        ? -output-file <value> : (test and verify only) Path to write full test results to a file. Format is inferred from the file extension (.json, .yaml, .toon, .xml).
OUT|h6|        ? -export-deps <0/1> : If specified, controls whether dependencies are exported. If omitted, defaults to the value of the #EXPORTDEPENDENCIES in lifecycle class. This modifier is only used in "Package" and "Publish" lifecycles.
OUT|h6|        ? -skip-sync : In developer mode, skips recording the file hashes that sync uses to detect changes during the compile phase. The next sync of the module records them instead.
