OUT|h7|init [flags]
OUT|h7|    ? Alias: initialize
OUT|h7|    ? Description: Configures new namespace for use of package manager (interactive). This sets up the
OUT|h7|local cache and allows for configuration of studio extensions for source control and
OUT|h7|the package manager itself. In the case of Perforce-based source control, prompts are
OUT|h7|included for username, password, Perforce workspace, and a few other settings.
OUT|h7|
OUT|h7|    ? Flags:
OUT|h7|        ? -noprompt, -quiet, -q : If specified, no prompts will be shown.
OUT|h7|        ? -zpm, -cli : If specified, the zpm command will be configured.
OUT|h7|        ? -extension, -ext <value> : Generic extension (e.g., extension class for source control) to configure for the current namespace 
OUT|h7|        ? -menuextension, -menuext <value> : Server menu extension (Package Manager extension for server menus such as Studio, vscode menu, etc.) to configure for the current namespace
OUT|h7|
OUT|h7|
OUT|h7|install [flags] <module> [<version>]
OUT|h7|    ? Description: Installs a module available in a configured repository.
OUT|h7|
OUT|h7|    ? Arguments
OUT|h7|        ? module: Name of module to install
OUT|h7|        ? version: Version (or version expression) of module to install; defaults to the latest available if unspecified.
OUT|h7|
OUT|h7|    ? Flags:
OUT|h7|        ? -env, -e <value> : Semicolon separated paths to the environment files in json format. See wiki for details.
OUT|h7|        ? -latest, -l : Installs the latest available version of this module without prompting the user.
OUT|h7|        ? -prompt, -p : Prompts user which version to install, if more then one found.
OUT|h7|        ? -dev : Sets the DeveloperMode flag for the module's lifecycle. Key consequences of this are that ^Sources will be configured for resources in the module, and installer methods will be called with the dev mode flag set. In addition, transactions will not be rolled back if installation fails.
OUT|h7|        ? -quiet, -q : Produces minimal output from the command.
OUT|h7|        ? -verbose, -v : Produces verbose output from the command.
OUT|h7|        ? -keywords, -k <value> : Searches for modules matching some set of keywords.
OUT|h7|        ? -bypass-py-deps : Skip installing python dependencies
OUT|h7|        ? -extra-pip-flags <value> : Extra flags to pass to pip when installing python dependencies. Surround the flags (and values) with quotes if spaces are present. Default flags are "--target <target> --python-version <pyversion> --only-binary=:all:".
OUT|h7|        ? -synchronous : DEPRECATED. Dependencies are now always loaded synchronously with independent lifecycle phases doing their own multi-threading as needed.
OUT|h7|        ? -force, -f : Allows the user to install a newer version of an existing module without running update steps.
OUT|h7|        ? -create-lockfile, -lock : Upon install, creates/updates the module's lock file.
OUT|h7|        ? -skip-sync : In developer mode, skips recording the file hashes that sync uses to detect changes. The next sync of the module records them instead.
OUT|h7|
OUT|h7|    ? Examples
OUT|h7|        ?     install HS.JSON 1.x        
OUT|h7|            Installs the most recent 1.x version of HS.JSON available in any configured repository in the current namespace.
OUT|h7|        ?     install registry/HS.JSON        
OUT|h7|            Installs the most recent version of HS.JSON from the `registry` repository.
OUT|h7|        ?     install -bypass-py-deps HS.JSON        
OUT|h7|            Installs HS.JSON without installing python dependencies
OUT|h7|        ?     install -extra-pip-flags "--timeout 30" HS.JSON        
OUT|h7|            Installs HS.JSON but set pip timeout to 30 seconds
OUT|h7|        ? install -env /path/to/env1.json;/path/to/env2.json example-package        
OUT|h7|            Installs example-package using the /path/to/env1.json and /path/to/env2.json as the install time configuration
OUT|h7|
OUT|h7|
OUT|h7|list-dependents [flags] <module> [<version>]
OUT|h7|    ? Alias: dependents
OUT|h7|    ? Description:     Lists modules dependent on the specified module.
OUT|h7|
OUT|h7|    ? Arguments
OUT|h7|        ? module: Name of module for which dependent modules will be found
OUT|h7|        ? version: Version of the module for which dependent modules will be found (in all configured repositories)
OUT|h7|
OUT|h7|    ? Flags:
OUT|h7|        ? -tree, -t : If specified, show as a tree (rather than a flattened list)
OUT|h7|        ? -repos, -r <value> : Comma-separated list of repository names to search in. If unspecified, the version of the module in the current namespace will be used instead.
OUT|h7|
OUT|h7|    ? Examples
OUT|h7|        ?     list-dependents HS.JSON        
OUT|h7|            Lists all currently-installed modules dependent on all 'HS.JSON' versions
OUT|h7|        ?     list-dependents -repos AppModules HS.JSON        
OUT|h7|            Lists all currently-installed modules in the 'AppModules' repository dependent on all 'HS.JSON' versions
OUT|h7|        ?     dependents -t -r AppModules HS.JSON 0.0.1+snapshot        
OUT|h7|            Lists all currently-installed modules in the 'AppModules' repository dependent on 'HS.JSON' version '0.0.1+snapshot', as a tree
OUT|h7|
OUT|h7|
OUT|h7|list-installed [flags] [<searchString>]
OUT|h7|    ? Alias: list
OUT|h7|    ? Description:     Lists modules installed in the current namespace.
OUT|h7|
OUT|h7|    ? Arguments
OUT|h7|        ? searchString: Search string, * can be used.
OUT|h7|
OUT|h7|    ? Flags:
OUT|h7|        ? -tree, -t : If specified, show dependency tree for installed modules.
OUT|h7|        ? -description, -d : Additional information is displayed for each module.
OUT|h7|        ? -showsource, -ss : If specified, show dependency list with local source for installed modules.
OUT|h7|        ? -showtime, -st : If specified, show the time of last update for each module
OUT|h7|        ? -showupstream, -su : If specified, show the latest version for each module in configured repos if it's different than the local version.
OUT|h7|        ? -repository, -repo <value> : If specified, only show modules installed that belong to the provided repository.
OUT|h7|        ? -python, -py : If specified, lists installed Embedded Python libraries instead of IPM modules.
OUT|h7|        ? -sort, -s <value> : Sort the list. Default (no -s) is ascending by name with IPM on top. Options: name, name-desc, date, date-desc, version, version-desc. Shortforms: n, d, v; append -d for descending (e.g. n-d, d-d, v-d). Has no effect with -tree or -python.
OUT|h7|
OUT|h7|    ? Examples
OUT|h7|        ?     list-installed -tree        
OUT|h7|            Shows all installed modules in tree format.
OUT|h7|        ?     list -python        
OUT|h7|            Shows all installed Python packages.
OUT|h7|        ?     list -s [name|date|version]        
OUT|h7|            Sort by name ascending (default). Shortforms: n=name, d=date, v=version. Append -d for descending.
OUT|h7|        ?     list -s name-desc        
OUT|h7|            Sort by name descending
OUT|h7|        ?     list -s d-d        
OUT|h7|            Sort by installation date descending
OUT|h7|
OUT|h7|
OUT|h7|load [flags] <path>
OUT|h7|    ? Description: Loads a module from the specified directory into the current namespace.
OUT|h7|Dependencies are also loaded automatically, provided that they can be found in
OUT|h7|repositories configured with the 'repo' command.
OUT|h7|
OUT|h7|    ? Arguments
OUT|h7|        ? path: Directory on the local filesystem, containing a file named module.xml
OUT|h7|
OUT|h7|    ? Flags:
OUT|h7|        ? -env, -e <value> : Semicolon separated paths to the environment files in json format. See wiki for details.
OUT|h7|        ? -branch, -b <value> : The name of the branch in the repository
OUT|h7|        ? -dev : Sets the DeveloperMode flag for the module's lifecycle. Key consequences of this are that ^Sources will be configured for resources in the module, and installer methods will be called with the dev mode flag set. In addition, transactions will not be rolled back if installation fails.
OUT|h7|        ? -nodev : Disables the DeveloperMode flag for the module's lifecycle.
OUT|h7|        ? -quiet, -q : Produces minimal output from the command.
OUT|h7|        ? -verbose, -v : Produces verbose output from the command.
OUT|h7|        ? -bypass-py-deps : Skip installing python dependencies
OUT|h7|        ? -extra-pip-flags <value> : Extra flags to pass to pip when installing python dependencies. Surround the flags (and values) with quotes if spaces are present. Default flags are "--target <target> --python-version <pyversion> --only-binary=:all:".
OUT|h7|        ? -synchronous : DEPRECATED. Dependencies are now always loaded synchronously with independent lifecycle phases doing their own multi-threading as needed.
OUT|h7|        ? -force, -f : Allows the user to load a newer version of an existing module without running update steps.
OUT|h7|        ? -create-lockfile, -lock : Upon load, creates/updates the module's lock file.
OUT|h7|        ? -skip-sync : In developer mode, skips recording the file hashes that sync uses to detect changes. The next sync of the module records them instead.
OUT|h7|
OUT|h7|    ? Examples
OUT|h7|        ? load C:\module\root\path\
OUT|h7|load C:\module\root\path\module-0.0.1.tgz
OUT|h7|load C:\module\root\path\module-0.0.1.tar.gz        
OUT|h7|            Loads the module described in C:\module\root\path\module.xml
OUT|h7|        ? load -dev -verbose C:\module\root\path\
OUT|h7|load -dev -verbose C:\module\root\path\module-0.0.1.tgz        
OUT|h7|            Loads the module described in C:\module\root\path\module.xml in developer mode and with verbose output.
OUT|h7|        ? load https://github.com/user/repository.git
OUT|h7|load https://github.com/user/repository.git -branch feature-1        
OUT|h7|            Loads the module in branch feature-1 from remote repository https://github.com/user/repository.git.
OUT|h7|        ?     load -bypass-py-deps C:\module\root\path\        
OUT|h7|            Loads the module described in C:\module\root\path\module.xml without installing python dependencies
OUT|h7|        ?     load -extra-pip-flags "--timeout 30" C:\module\root\path\        
OUT|h7|            Loads the module described in C:\module\root\path\module.xml but set pip timeout to 30 seconds
OUT|h7|        ? load C:\module\root\path -env C:\path\to\env1.json;C:\path\to\env2.json        
OUT|h7|            Loads the module described in C:\module\root\path\module.xml using the C:\path\to\env.json1 and C:\path\to\env.json2 as the install time configuration
OUT|h7|
OUT|h7|
OUT|h7|makedeployed [flags] <module>
OUT|h7|    ? Description: This command is an alias for `module-action module-name makedeployed`
OUT|h7|
OUT|h7|    ? Arguments
OUT|h7|        ? module: Name of module on which to perform reload action
OUT|h7|
OUT|h7|    ? Flags:
OUT|h7|        ? -env, -e <value> : Semicolon separated paths to the environment files in json format. See wiki for details.
OUT|h7|        ? -only, -o : Only runs the specified phase(s), rather than also running predecessors.
OUT|h7|        ? -dev : Sets the DeveloperMode flag for the module's lifecycle. Key consequences of this are that ^Sources will be configured for resources in the module, and installer methods will be called with the dev mode flag set.
OUT|h7|        ? -quiet, -q : Produces minimal output. For test and verify: suppresses build/install noise while still showing the result summary and any failures.
OUT|h7|        ? -verbose, -v : Produces verbose output. For test and verify: shows per-method results in the result summary when combined with -f; without -f only the summary is shown.
OUT|h7|        ? -format, -f <json/yaml/toon> : (test and verify only) One-shot output format override for the test result summary. Overrides the persistent default set via 'config set TestReportFormat <format>'. When neither -f nor config is set, the default summary and legacy failure lines are shown. Use 'config delete TestReportFormat' to revert to default.
OUT|h7|        ? -output-file <value> : (test and verify only) Path to write full test results to a file. Format is inferred from the file extension (.json, .yaml, .toon, .xml).
OUT|h7|        ? -export-deps <0/1> : If specified, controls whether dependencies are exported. If omitted, defaults to the value of the #EXPORTDEPENDENCIES in lifecycle class. This modifier is only used in "Package" and "Publish" lifecycles.
OUT|h7|        ? -skip-sync : In developer mode, skips recording the file hashes that sync uses to detect changes during the compile phase. The next sync of the module records them instead.
OUT|h7|        ? -recurse, -r : Runs the specified phase (makedeployed) on the module and all of its dependencies.
OUT|h7|
OUT|h7|
OUT|h7|module-action <module> <actions> [flags]
OUT|h7|    ? Description: Performs operations on modules - compiling, running tests, packaging/registering, etc.
OUT|h7|You can use this by starting a command with the module name.
OUT|h7|Note that flags appear *after* all actions.
OUT|h7|
OUT|h7|The standard lifecycle phases are:
OUT|h7|* clean: removes all dependencies that are not required by other installed modules and
OUT|h7|their resources. Dependencies required by other modules will also be removed if the -DClean.Force=1 flag is specified.
OUT|h7|* reload: pulls module source code into the namespace from disk. Does not compile.
OUT|h7|* validate: ensures that module resource processor attributes are valid, and that the
OUT|h7|resources exported to the filesystem (and possible to source control) are consistent
OUT|h7|with what is in the database.
OUT|h7|* compile: compiles all resources within the module.
OUT|h7|* activate: performs post-compilation installation/configuration steps.
OUT|h7|* makedeployed: deploys resources within the module for which deployment is enabled.
OUT|h7|* document: regenerates the API documentation for the module
OUT|h7|* test: runs any unit tests associated with the module, in the current namespace.
OUT|h7|* package: exports the module's resources and bundles them into a module artifact (.tgz file).
OUT|h7|* verify: installs that artifact in a separate namespace, then runs integration tests (if any).
OUT|h7|* register: saves that artifact into the current namespace's module cache.
OUT|h7|This is accessible to other instances configured to look at the current namespace as a
OUT|h7|module repository.
OUT|h7|* publish: saves that artifact to the repository for which deployment is enabled.
OUT|h7|Currently, there may only be one of these per namespace.
OUT|h7|* applyupdatesteps: run any update steps for the module to become updated to the latest available version.
OUT|h7|Can also specify desired version to update to.
OUT|h7|
OUT|h7|    ? Arguments
OUT|h7|        ? module: Name of module on which to perform lifecycle actions
OUT|h7|        ? actions: Space-delimited list of module lifecycle phases to run
OUT|h7|
OUT|h7|    ? Flags:
OUT|h7|        ? -env, -e <value> : Semicolon separated paths to the environment files in json format. See wiki for details.
OUT|h7|        ? -only, -o : Only runs the specified phase(s), rather than also running predecessors.
OUT|h7|        ? -dev : Sets the DeveloperMode flag for the module's lifecycle. Key consequences of this are that ^Sources will be configured for resources in the module, and installer methods will be called with the dev mode flag set.
OUT|h7|        ? -quiet, -q : Produces minimal output. For test and verify: suppresses build/install noise while still showing the result summary and any failures.
OUT|h7|        ? -verbose, -v : Produces verbose output. For test and verify: shows per-method results in the result summary when combined with -f; without -f only the summary is shown.
OUT|h7|        ? -format, -f <json/yaml/toon> : (test and verify only) One-shot output format override for the test result summary. Overrides the persistent default set via 'config set TestReportFormat <format>'. When neither -f nor config is set, the default summary and legacy failure lines are shown. Use 'config delete TestReportFormat' to revert to default.
OUT|h7|        ? -output-file <value> : (test and verify only) Path to write full test results to a file. Format is inferred from the file extension (.json, .yaml, .toon, .xml).
OUT|h7|        ? -export-deps <0/1> : If specified, controls whether dependencies are exported. If omitted, defaults to the value of the #EXPORTDEPENDENCIES in lifecycle class. This modifier is only used in "Package" and "Publish" lifecycles.
OUT|h7|        ? -skip-sync : In developer mode, skips recording the file hashes that sync uses to detect changes during the compile phase. The next sync of the module records them instead.
OUT|h7|        ? -path, -p <value> : the specified path to export package.
OUT|h7|        ? -export-python-deps : If specified, exports Python dependencies. If omitted, defaults to false.
OUT|h7|        ? -include-tests : If specified, includes test-scoped resources in the package.
OUT|h7|        ? -repo, -r : Namespace-unique name of repository for the module to publish to (if deployment is enabled)
OUT|h7|        ? -use-external-name, -use-ext : Publish the package under the <ExternalName> of the package. If ExternalName is unspecified or illegal, an error will be thrown.
OUT|h7|        ? -recurse, -r : Runs the specified phase (makedeployed) on the module and all of its dependencies.
OUT|h7|
OUT|h7|    ? Examples
OUT|h7|        ? module-action MyModuleName compile        
OUT|h7|            Compiles the module named "MyModuleName"
OUT|h7|        ? MyModuleName clean register        
OUT|h7|            Performs multiple actions on the module named "MyModuleName"; "clean" deletes all of its dependenices, and "install" will then re-download them, package the module, and register it in the current namespace's module cache.
OUT|h7|        ? MyUIModule compile -v -DUIFW.force=42        
OUT|h7|            Compiles the module named MyUIModule with verbose output and pParams("UIFW","force") (passed to all lifecycle phases) set to 42.
OUT|h7|        ? MyModule verify -env /path/to/env.json        
OUT|h7|            Verify the module MyModule with the env file /path/to/env.json. The module is responsible for accessing the data using %IPM.General.EnvironmentConfig:GetArg()
OUT|h7|
OUT|h7|
OUT|h7|module-version [flags] <module> [<version>]
OUT|h7|    ? Alias: modver
OUT|h7|    ? Description:         Updates the version of the module in the current namespace.
OUT|h7|    
OUT|h7|
OUT|h7|    ? Arguments
OUT|h7|        ? module: Name of the module to update the version for.
OUT|h7|        ? version: Either a valid SemVer string, or 'major', 'minor', 'patch'. If it's 'major', 'minor', or 'patch', will bump the cooresponding part of SemVer.
OUT|h7|
OUT|h7|    ? Flags:
OUT|h7|        ? -prerelease, -p <value> : Set the prerelease to a specific value
OUT|h7|        ? -build, -b <value> : Set the build to a specific value
OUT|h7|        ? -force : Permit the version to be downgraded. By default, trying to downgrade the version will fail
OUT|h7|        ? -quiet, -q : Suppress output (except for errors)
OUT|h7|
OUT|h7|    ? Examples
OUT|h7|        ?         module-version HS.JSON 1.2.3
OUT|h7|            
OUT|h7|            Sets the version of the module HS.JSON to 1.2.3
OUT|h7|        ?         module-version HS.JSON major
OUT|h7|            
OUT|h7|            Bump the major version the module HS.JSON. If current version is 1.2.3, the new version will be 2.0.0
OUT|h7|        ?         module-version HS.JSON minor -prerelease alpha -build xyz
OUT|h7|            
OUT|h7|            Bump the minor version and set prerelease to 'alpha' and build to 'xyz'. If current version is 1.2.3, the new version will be 1.3.0-alpha+xyz
OUT|h7|        ?         module-version HS.JSON 1.2.3 -force
OUT|h7|            
OUT|h7|            Downgrade the version to 1.2.3. If the current version is 1.3.0, this will still work because the force flag is set.
OUT|h7|
OUT|h7|
OUT|h7|namespace [flags] [<name>]
OUT|h7|    ? Alias: zn
OUT|h7|    ? Description: See list modules in namespace and go to the namespace
OUT|h7|
OUT|h7|    ? Arguments
OUT|h7|        ? name: Name namespace, * or context name*
OUT|h7|
OUT|h7|    ? Flags:
OUT|h7|        ? -description, -d : Shows description for each module.
OUT|h7|
OUT|h7|    ? Examples
OUT|h7|        ? zn *        
OUT|h7|            Show all modules in all namespaces
OUT|h7|        ? zn sql*        
OUT|h7|            Show all modules in namespaces by context
OUT|h7|
OUT|h7|
OUT|h7|orphans [flags]
OUT|h7|    ? Description:     Lists resources in the current namespace's default code database that are not part of any module.
OUT|h7|
OUT|h7|    ? Flags:
OUT|h7|        ? -type, -t <value> : Type (e.g., extension) of resource to show; if unspecified, all types are included.
OUT|h7|
OUT|h7|    ? Examples
OUT|h7|        ?     orphans -type CLS        
OUT|h7|            List resources that are classes that are not part of any module in the current namespace
OUT|h7|
OUT|h7|
OUT|h7|package [flags] <module>
OUT|h7|    ? Description: This command is an alias for `module-action module-name package`
OUT|h7|
OUT|h7|    ? Arguments
OUT|h7|        ? module: Name of module on which to perform package actions
OUT|h7|
OUT|h7|    ? Flags:
OUT|h7|        ? -env, -e <value> : Semicolon separated paths to the environment files in json format. See wiki for details.
OUT|h7|        ? -only, -o : Only runs the specified phase(s), rather than also running predecessors.
OUT|h7|        ? -dev : Sets the DeveloperMode flag for the module's lifecycle. Key consequences of this are that ^Sources will be configured for resources in the module, and installer methods will be called with the dev mode flag set.
OUT|h7|        ? -quiet, -q : Produces minimal output. For test and verify: suppresses build/install noise while still showing the result summary and any failures.
OUT|h7|        ? -verbose, -v : Produces verbose output. For test and verify: shows per-method results in the result summary when combined with -f; without -f only the summary is shown.
OUT|h7|        ? -format, -f <json/yaml/toon> : (test and verify only) One-shot output format override for the test result summary. Overrides the persistent default set via 'config set TestReportFormat <format>'. When neither -f nor config is set, the default summary and legacy failure lines are shown. Use 'config delete TestReportFormat' to revert to default.
OUT|h7|        ? -output-file <value> : (test and verify only) Path to write full test results to a file. Format is inferred from the file extension (.json, .yaml, .toon, .xml).
OUT|h7|        ? -skip-sync : In developer mode, skips recording the file hashes that sync uses to detect changes during the compile phase. The next sync of the module records them instead.
OUT|h7|        ? -path, -p <value> : the specified path to export package.
OUT|h7|        ? -export-deps <0/1> : If specified, controls whether dependencies are exported. If omitted, defaults to the value of the #EXPORTDEPENDENCIES in lifecycle class. This modifier is only used in "Package" and "Publish" lifecycles.
OUT|h7|        ? -export-python-deps : If specified, exports Python dependencies. If omitted, defaults to false.
OUT|h7|        ? -include-tests : If specified, includes test-scoped resources in the package.
OUT|h7|
OUT|h7|
OUT|h7|publish [flags] <module>
OUT|h7|    ? Description: This command is an alias for `module-action module-name publish`
OUT|h7|
OUT|h7|    ? Arguments
OUT|h7|        ? module: Name of module on which to perform publish actions
OUT|h7|
OUT|h7|    ? Flags:
OUT|h7|        ? -env, -e <value> : Semicolon separated paths to the environment files in json format. See wiki for details.
OUT|h7|        ? -only, -o : Only runs the specified phase(s), rather than also running predecessors.
OUT|h7|        ? -dev : Sets the DeveloperMode flag for the module's lifecycle. Key consequences of this are that ^Sources will be configured for resources in the module, and installer methods will be called with the dev mode flag set.
OUT|h7|        ? -quiet, -q : Produces minimal output. For test and verify: suppresses build/install noise while still showing the result summary and any failures.
OUT|h7|        ? -verbose, -v : Produces verbose output. For test and verify: shows per-method results in the result summary when combined with -f; without -f only the summary is shown.
OUT|h7|        ? -format, -f <json/yaml/toon> : (test and verify only) One-shot output format override for the test result summary. Overrides the persistent default set via 'config set TestReportFormat <format>'. When neither -f nor config is set, the default summary and legacy failure lines are shown. Use 'config delete TestReportFormat' to revert to default.
OUT|h7|        ? -output-file <value> : (test and verify only) Path to write full test results to a file. Format is inferred from the file extension (.json, .yaml, .toon, .xml).
OUT|h7|        ? -export-deps <0/1> : If specified, controls whether dependencies are exported. If omitted, defaults to the value of the #EXPORTDEPENDENCIES in lifecycle class. This modifier is only used in "Package" and "Publish" lifecycles.
OUT|h7|        ? -skip-sync : In developer mode, skips recording the file hashes that sync uses to detect changes during the compile phase. The next sync of the module records them instead.
OUT|h7|        ? -repo, -r : Namespace-unique name of repository for the module to publish to (if deployment is enabled)
OUT|h7|        ? -use-external-name, -use-ext : Publish the package under the <ExternalName> of the package. If ExternalName is unspecified or illegal, an error will be thrown.
OUT|h7|        ? -export-python-deps : If specified, exports Python dependencies. If omitted, defaults to false.
OUT|h7|        ? -include-tests : If specified, includes test-scoped resources in the package.
OUT|h7|
OUT|h7|
OUT|h7|quit
OUT|h7|    ? Aliases: q, exit
OUT|h7|    ? Description: Exits the package manager shell.
OUT|h7|
OUT|h7|
OUT|h7|reinstall [flags] <module>
OUT|h7|    ? Description:     Reinstalls an already-installed module from the latest version available in a configured
OUT|h7|    repository. By default, updates dependencies of that module as well.
OUT|h7|
OUT|h7|    ? Arguments
OUT|h7|        ? module: Name of module to reinstall
OUT|h7|
OUT|h7|    ? Flags:
OUT|h7|        ? -dev : Sets the DeveloperMode flag for the module's lifecycle. Key consequences of this are that ^Sources will be configured for resources in the module, and installer methods will be called with the dev mode flag set.
OUT|h7|        ? -env, -e <value> : Semicolon separated paths to the environment files in json format. See wiki for details.
OUT|h7|        ? -quiet, -q : Produces minimal output from the command.
OUT|h7|        ? -verbose, -v : Produces verbose output from the command.
OUT|h7|        ? -shallow, -s : Suppresses updating of dependencies with '+snapshot' versions.
OUT|h7|        ? -bypass-py-deps : Skip installing python dependencies
OUT|h7|        ? -extra-pip-flags <value> : Extra flags to pass to pip when installing python dependencies. Surround the flags (and values) with quotes if spaces are present. Default flags are "--target <target> --python-version <pyversion> --only-binary=:all:".
OUT|h7|        ? -skip-sync : In developer mode, skips recording the file hashes that sync uses to detect changes. The next sync of the module records them instead.
OUT|h7|
OUT|h7|    ? Examples
OUT|h7|        ?     reinstall -dev MyTestModule        
OUT|h7|            Reinstalls the currently-installed version of the MyTestModule module, in development mode
OUT|h7|        ?     reinstall -bypass-py-deps MyTestModule        
OUT|h7|            Reinstalls the currently-installed vresion of the MyTestModule module without reinstalling python dependencies
OUT|h7|        ?     reinstall -extra-pip-flags "--timeout 30" MyTestModule        
OUT|h7|            Reinstalls MyTestModule but set pip timeout to 30 seconds
OUT|h7|        ? reinstall -env /path/to/env1.json;/path/to/env2.json example-package        
OUT|h7|            Reinstalls example-package using the /path/to/env1.json and /path/to/env2.json as the install time configuration
OUT|h7|
OUT|h7|
OUT|h7|reload [flags] <module>
OUT|h7|    ? Description: This command is an alias for `module-action module-name reload`
OUT|h7|
OUT|h7|    ? Arguments
OUT|h7|        ? module: Name of module on which to perform reload action
OUT|h7|
OUT|h7|    ? Flags:
OUT|h7|        ? -env, -e <value> : Semicolon separated paths to the environment files in json format. See wiki for details.
OUT|h7|        ? -only, -o : Only runs the specified phase(s), rather than also running predecessors.
OUT|h7|        ? -dev : Sets the DeveloperMode flag for the module's lifecycle. Key consequences of this are that ^Sources will be configured for resources in the module, and installer methods will be called with the dev mode flag set.
OUT|h7|        ? -quiet, -q : Produces minimal output. For test and verify: suppresses build/install noise while still showing the result summary and any failures.
OUT|h7|        ? -verbose, -v : Produces verbose output. For test and verify: shows per-method results in the result summary when combined with -f; without -f only the summary is shown.
OUT|h7|        ? -format, -f <json/yaml/toon> : (test and verify only) One-shot output format override for the test result summary. Overrides the persistent default set via 'config set TestReportFormat <format>'. When neither -f nor config is set, the default summary and legacy failure lines are shown. Use 'config delete TestReportFormat' to revert to default.
OUT|h7|        ? -output-file <value> : (test and verify only) Path to write full test results to a file. Format is inferred from the file extension (.json, .yaml, .toon, .xml).
OUT|h7|        ? -export-deps <0/1> : If specified, controls whether dependencies are exported. If omitted, defaults to the value of the #EXPORTDEPENDENCIES in lifecycle class. This modifier is only used in "Package" and "Publish" lifecycles.
OUT|h7|        ? -skip-sync : In developer mode, skips recording the file hashes that sync uses to detect changes during the compile phase. The next sync of the module records them instead.
OUT|h7|
OUT|h7|
OUT|h7|repo [flags]
OUT|h7|    ? Alias: repository
OUT|h7|    ? Description: Configures the current namespace to search for modules on a remote server or on the
OUT|h7|local filesystem.
OUT|h7|
OUT|h7|    ? Flags:
OUT|h7|        ? -delete-all : Deletes all repositories (possibly subject to a type filter: e.g -filesystem, -remote, -local)
OUT|h7|        ? -list : Lists all repositories (possibly subject to a type filter: e.g. -filesystem, -remote, -local)
OUT|h7|        ? -list-modules, -modules, -listmodules : List modules available in the specified repository (-n\[ame]), or in all configured repositories if no repository was specified.
OUT|h7|        ? -info : Shows configuration details for the current namespace as a remote repository.
OUT|h7|        ? -copy-from <value> : Copies repository configuration from a different (specified) namespace
OUT|h7|        ? -name, -n <value> : Namespace-unique name for the module
OUT|h7|        ? -delete : Deletes the current namespace's reference to the named repository
OUT|h7|        ? -publish <0/1> : When configuring a remote repository, specifies that publishing of packages to the repository is allowed. When configuring the current namespace with -enable, specifies that publishing is also enabled. 
OUT|h7|        ? -enable : For any repository, specifies that it can be used.
OUT|h7|        ? -disable : For any repository, specifies that it can not be used.
OUT|h7|        ? -snapshots, -s <0/1> : For any repository, specifies that it should be used to look for snapshot builds (i.e., those with a semantic version ending in '+snapshot', indicating a 'latest' build of a particular version).
OUT|h7|        ? -prereleases, -pre <0/1> : For any repository, specifies that it should be used to look for prerelease software
OUT|h7|        ? -read-only, -ro <0/1> : For any repository, specifies that modules installed from it are ALWAYS installed in non-developer mode, effectively making them read only
OUT|h7|        ? -type, -t <value> : Subclass of %IPM.Repo.Definition to create/modify/delete or implementation of %IPM.Repo.IPackageService or %IPM.Repo.IPublishService to enable/disable
OUT|h7|
OUT|h7|    ? Examples
OUT|h7|        ? repo -list        
OUT|h7|            List all repositories
OUT|h7|        ? repo -delete-all        
OUT|h7|            Delete all repositories
OUT|h7|        ? repo -n LocalFiles -delete        
OUT|h7|            Delete the repository named "LocalFiles"
OUT|h7|        ? repo -n AppModules -list-modules        
OUT|h7|            Lists all modules (and versions) available from the repository named "AppModules"
OUT|h7|        ? repo -enable -app /csp/mynamespace -publish 1        
OUT|h7|            Enables package manager web services in this namespace via the /csp/mynamespace/ web application, including web services for publishing. Note that security (e.g., authentication settings, required role) should be configured separately as part of this web application.
OUT|h7|
OUT|h7|    ? Filesystem repository:
OUT|h7|        ? -filesystem, -f, -fs : Create/update a filesystem repository
OUT|h7|        ? -depth, -d <value> : For filesystem repositories, specifies the depth (number of folders underneath the root) to search for files named module.xml
OUT|h7|        ? -path, -p <value> : For filesystem repositories, specifies the path to the root directory
OUT|h7|
OUT|h7|    ? Examples
OUT|h7|        ? repo -name LocalFiles -snapshots 1 -fs -depth 2 -path C:\MyWorkspace\RootModuleDir\        
OUT|h7|            Create a repository pointing to the filesystem with name LocalFiles, accepting snapshots, looking for files named module.xml up to 2 directories deep in C:\MyWorkspace\RootModuleDir\
OUT|h7|        ? repo -delete-all        
OUT|h7|            Delete all repositories
OUT|h7|        ? repo -n LocalFiles -delete        
OUT|h7|            Delete the repository named "LocalFiles"
OUT|h7|        ? repo -n AppModules -list-modules        
OUT|h7|            Lists all modules (and versions) available from the repository named "AppModules"
OUT|h7|        ? repo -enable -app /csp/mynamespace -publish 1        
OUT|h7|            Enables package manager web services in this namespace via the /csp/mynamespace/ web application, including web services for publishing. Note that security (e.g., authentication settings, required role) should be configured separately as part of this web application.
OUT|h7|
OUT|h7|    ? HTTP(s) repository:
OUT|h7|        ? -url <value> : For HTTP(s) repositories, specifies the URL for package retrieval web services.
OUT|h7|        ? -publish-url <value> : For HTTP(s) repositories, specifies the URL for package publishing web services.
OUT|h7|        ? -username, -user <value> : For HTTP(s) repositories, specifies the username to use when connecting.
OUT|h7|        ? -password, -pass <value> : For HTTP(s) repositories, specifies the password to use when connecting.
OUT|h7|        ? -password-stdin, -pass-stdin : For HTTP(s) repositories, specifies the password to use when connecting, but prompt user in secret input terminal mode.
OUT|h7|        ? -password-env, -pass-env <value> : For HTTP(s) repositories, reads the password from the named environment variable.
OUT|h7|        ? -token <value> : For HTTP(s) repositories, specifies the key to use when connecting.
OUT|h7|        ? -token-env <value> : For HTTP(s) repositories, reads the token from the named environment variable.
OUT|h7|        ? -token-auth-method <value> : Enum of { basic, bearer, apiKey }. Defaults to apiKey. If set to 'basic', token will be included as 'Authorization: Basic <token>'. If set to 'bearer', token will be included as 'Authorization: Bearer <token>'. If set to 'apiKey', token will be included as 'apiKey: <token>'
OUT|h7|        ? -sslconfig, -ssl <value> : For HTTP(s) repositories, specifies the SSL configuration name to use when connecting.
OUT|h7|
OUT|h7|    ? Examples
OUT|h7|        ? repo -name LocalFiles -snapshots 1 -fs -depth 2 -path C:\MyWorkspace\RootModuleDir\        
OUT|h7|            Create a repository pointing to the filesystem with name LocalFiles, accepting snapshots, looking for files named module.xml up to 2 directories deep in C:\MyWorkspace\RootModuleDir\
OUT|h7|        ? repo -delete-all        
OUT|h7|            Delete all repositories
OUT|h7|        ? repo -n LocalFiles -delete        
OUT|h7|            Delete the repository named "LocalFiles"
OUT|h7|        ? repo -n AppModules -list-modules        
OUT|h7|            Lists all modules (and versions) available from the repository named "AppModules"
OUT|h7|        ? repo -enable -app /csp/mynamespace -publish 1        
OUT|h7|            Enables package manager web services in this namespace via the /csp/mynamespace/ web application, including web services for publishing. Note that security (e.g., authentication settings, required role) should be configured separately as part of this web application.
OUT|h7|
OUT|h7|    ? ORAS repository:
OUT|h7|        ? -oras, -o : Create/update an ORAS server repository
OUT|h7|        ? -namespace, -ns <value> : For ORAS repositories, specifies the registry namespace, i.e. the fully specified path to a package is <url>/<namespace>/<package>. Note, this is not the IRIS namespace, but a namespace in the sense of a (sub)directory in the registry's storage system. For example, if in a custom managed registry, the base url is 'registry.company.com', and the package is 'objectscript-example', but it will be stored at 'registry.company.com/packages/ipm/objectscript-example', then the namespace will be 'packages/ipm'.
OUT|h7|
OUT|h7|    ? Examples
OUT|h7|        ? repo -name LocalFiles -snapshots 1 -fs -depth 2 -path C:\MyWorkspace\RootModuleDir\        
OUT|h7|            Create a repository pointing to the filesystem with name LocalFiles, accepting snapshots, looking for files named module.xml up to 2 directories deep in C:\MyWorkspace\RootModuleDir\
OUT|h7|        ? repo -delete-all        
OUT|h7|            Delete all repositories
OUT|h7|        ? repo -n LocalFiles -delete        
OUT|h7|            Delete the repository named "LocalFiles"
OUT|h7|        ? repo -n AppModules -list-modules        
OUT|h7|            Lists all modules (and versions) available from the repository named "AppModules"
OUT|h7|        ? repo -enable -app /csp/mynamespace -publish 1        
OUT|h7|            Enables package manager web services in this namespace via the /csp/mynamespace/ web application, including web services for publishing. Note that security (e.g., authentication settings, required role) should be configured separately as part of this web application.
OUT|h7|
OUT|h7|    ? Remote repository:
OUT|h7|        ? -remote, -r : Create/update a remote server repository
OUT|h7|        ? -reset-defaults : Reset to default repository
OUT|h7|
OUT|h7|    ? Examples
OUT|h7|        ? repo -r -n registry -url https://test.pm.community.intersystems.com/registry/ -user "test" -pass "test"        
OUT|h7|            Switch to test registery
OUT|h7|        ? repo -r -n registry -reset-defaults        
OUT|h7|            Reset to default registry (pm.community.intersystems.com) and it is equivalent to a command
OUT|h7|        ? repo -r -n registry -url https://pm.community.intersystems.com/ -user "" -pass ""        
OUT|h7|             
OUT|h7|        ? repo -n AppModules -list-modules        
OUT|h7|            Lists all modules (and versions) available from the repository named "AppModules"
OUT|h7|        ? repo -enable -app /csp/mynamespace -publish 1        
OUT|h7|            Enables package manager web services in this namespace via the /csp/mynamespace/ web application, including web services for publishing. Note that security (e.g., authentication settings, required role) should be configured separately as part of this web application.
OUT|h7|
OUT|h7|
OUT|h7|run-from-file [flags] <file>
OUT|h7|    ? Alias: run
OUT|h7|    ? Description:         Imports either a text file or a JSON file that contains Package Manager Shell commands and runs them sequentially
OUT|h7|        in the current namespace.
OUT|h7|        Expected format if the file is a text file (with a .txt extension):
OUT|h7|        Each line of the file should contain a single command to be run
OUT|h7|        Expected format if the file is a JSON file (with a .json extension):
OUT|h7|        The file must contain a single array whose items are JSON objects matching the following format -
OUT|h7|        {
OUT|h7|            "command": "command name",
OUT|h7|            "parameters": { ... },
OUT|h7|            "modifiers": { ... },
OUT|h7|            "custom_modifiers": { ... }
OUT|h7|        }
OUT|h7|        where each nested object contains key-value pairs. Note that for modifiers that have no value associated with them
OUT|h7|        (such as -verbose), the value of the key-value pair for the modifier is ignored.
OUT|h7|
OUT|h7|    ? Arguments
OUT|h7|        ? file: File to import
OUT|h7|
OUT|h7|    ? Flags:
OUT|h7|        ? -dry-run, -d : Does not actually run the commands. Simply displays what would be run.
OUT|h7|
OUT|h7|    ? Examples
OUT|h7|        ? run-from-file C:\Temp\MyCommands.text, where contents of the file are as follows:
OUT|h7|    repo -name Test -p4 -path //Users/test/
OUT|h7|    install -dev TestModule -latest -DNoMapping=1 -DDeploy.Parameter="TESTDEPLOY"        
OUT|h7|            Run the commands present in the text file.
OUT|h7|        ? run C:\Temp\MyCommands.json, where contents of the file are as follows:
OUT|h7|    {
OUT|h7|        "commands": [
OUT|h7|            {
OUT|h7|                "command": "repo",
OUT|h7|                "modifiers": {
OUT|h7|                    "name": "Test",
OUT|h7|                    "p4": "",
OUT|h7|                    "path": "//Users/test/"
OUT|h7|                }
OUT|h7|            },
OUT|h7|            {
OUT|h7|                "command": "install",
OUT|h7|                "parameters": {
OUT|h7|                    "module": "TestModule"
OUT|h7|                },
OUT|h7|                "modifiers": {
OUT|h7|                    "dev": "",
OUT|h7|                    "latest": ""
OUT|h7|                },
OUT|h7|                "custom_modifiers": {
OUT|h7|                    "Deploy.Parameter": "TESTDEPLOY"
OUT|h7|                }
OUT|h7|            }
OUT|h7|        ]
OUT|h7|    }        
OUT|h7|            Run the commands present in the JSON file.
OUT|h7|
OUT|h7|
OUT|h7|search [flags] [<searchString>]
OUT|h7|    ? Alias: find
OUT|h7|    ? Description: Shows all modules matching the search string in all configured registries
OUT|h7|
OUT|h7|    ? Arguments
OUT|h7|        ? searchString: Search string, * can be used
OUT|h7|
OUT|h7|    ? Flags:
OUT|h7|        ? -show-repo, -r : Shows github repository only for each module.
OUT|h7|        ? -description, -d : Shows description for each module.
OUT|h7|        ? -versions : Shows all versions for each module.
OUT|h7|
OUT|h7|    ? Examples
OUT|h7|        ? search -versions zpm*        
OUT|h7|            Shows all versions of all modules starting with `zpm`
OUT|h7|        ? find -d *tools*        
OUT|h7|            Shows a description of all modules in the name of which there is a context
OUT|h7|
OUT|h7|
OUT|h7|show <module>
OUT|h7|    ? Description:     Shows an installed module's details, what it contains, and the modules it depends on or is required by. Use list-dependents for modules that depend on it transitively.
OUT|h7|
OUT|h7|    ? Arguments
OUT|h7|        ? module: Name of an installed module
OUT|h7|
OUT|h7|    ? Flags:
OUT|h7|
OUT|h7|    ? Examples
OUT|h7|        ?     show MyModule        
OUT|h7|            Shows the details, resources, and dependencies of the installed module MyModule.
OUT|h7|
OUT|h7|
OUT|h7|sync [flags] [<module>]
OUT|h7|    ? Description: Detects files changed on disk since the last load/sync for development-mode modules,
OUT|h7|routes each to its resource processor, and recompiles only what is stale.
OUT|h7|If no module is specified, syncs all modules in development mode.
OUT|h7|
OUT|h7|    ? Arguments
OUT|h7|        ? module: Name of module to sync (optional; if omitted, syncs all dev-mode modules)
OUT|h7|
OUT|h7|    ? Flags:
OUT|h7|        ? -delete : Also process deleted files (remove server documents and recompile dependents).
OUT|h7|        ? -test, -t : After sync, run test-phase unit tests that were added or modified.
OUT|h7|        ? -verbose, -v : Produces verbose output.
OUT|h7|
OUT|h7|
OUT|h7|test [flags] <module>
OUT|h7|    ? Description: This command is an alias for `module-action module-name test`
OUT|h7|
OUT|h7|    ? Arguments
OUT|h7|        ? module: Name of module on which to perform test action
OUT|h7|
OUT|h7|    ? Flags:
OUT|h7|        ? -env, -e <value> : Semicolon separated paths to the environment files in json format. See wiki for details.
OUT|h7|        ? -only, -o : Only runs the specified phase(s), rather than also running predecessors.
OUT|h7|        ? -dev : Sets the DeveloperMode flag for the module's lifecycle. Key consequences of this are that ^Sources will be configured for resources in the module, and installer methods will be called with the dev mode flag set.
OUT|h7|        ? -quiet, -q : Produces minimal output. For test and verify: suppresses build/install noise while still showing the result summary and any failures.
OUT|h7|        ? -verbose, -v : Produces verbose output. For test and verify: shows per-method results in the result summary when combined with -f; without -f only the summary is shown.
OUT|h7|        ? -format, -f <json/yaml/toon> : (test and verify only) One-shot output format override for the test result summary. Overrides the persistent default set via 'config set TestReportFormat <format>'. When neither -f nor config is set, the default summary and legacy failure lines are shown. Use 'config delete TestReportFormat' to revert to default.
OUT|h7|        ? -output-file <value> : (test and verify only) Path to write full test results to a file. Format is inferred from the file extension (.json, .yaml, .toon, .xml).
OUT|h7|        ? -export-deps <0/1> : If specified, controls whether dependencies are exported. If omitted, defaults to the value of the #EXPORTDEPENDENCIES in lifecycle class. This modifier is only used in "Package" and "Publish" lifecycles.
OUT|h7|        ? -skip-sync : In developer mode, skips recording the file hashes that sync uses to detect changes during the compile phase. The next sync of the module records them instead.
